# kernel-pin.env — the exact upstream coordinates this patch series is built against.
#
# Every value here was READ OUT of a specific upstream revision (recorded below),
# never assumed. Re-resolve with `scripts/preflight.sh` before changing anything.
# Consumed by scripts/apply.sh, the patch-apply CI workflow, and (downstream) the
# image-building-pipeline kernel-build-from-source stage.
#
# shellcheck shell=bash
# shellcheck disable=SC2034  # pure data file; every value is consumed by a sourcing caller

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Armbian build framework — the revision the mapping below was read from.
#
# (i) THIS BLOCK IS A MAPPING OBSERVATION, NOT A BUILD INSTRUCTION.
#
#     Armbian is consulted here for exactly one thing: which mainline
#     MAJOR.MINOR the rk3588 family associates with a branch name, and the
#     config / patch-directory names that follow from it. That is what makes
#     `rockchip64-7.2` a derived fact rather than a guess.
#
#     CeraLive does not run `compile.sh`. The kernel is built from source by
#     image-building-pipeline against KERNEL_TAG below, so nothing Armbian says
#     about branch availability can gate this repository — see (ii).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ARMBIAN_BUILD_REPO="https://github.com/armbian/build"
ARMBIAN_BUILD_REV="8af7de8d4117d058e3cda0907f08325aa809da9e"   # main @ 2026-08-17
ARMBIAN_BRANCH="bleedingedge"

# Both CeraLive RK3588 boards resolve to the SAME Armbian family, so one mapping
# covers both.
#
# (ii) `KERNEL_TARGET` IS QUOTED FOR THE RECORD AND IS DELIBERATELY NON-GATING.
#
#      Read at ARMBIAN_BUILD_REV:
#        config/boards/rock-5b-plus.conf    KERNEL_TARGET="vendor,current,edge"
#        config/boards/orangepi5-plus.conf  KERNEL_TARGET="current,edge,vendor"
#
#      Neither lists `bleedingedge`. That is a statement about which branches
#      Armbian's own board menu offers, not about which kernel the source can be
#      built against — and CeraLive never asks that menu. The image pipeline
#      clones KERNEL_TAG directly and builds it, so an absent menu entry costs us
#      nothing. `preflight.sh` therefore PRINTS both values and never fails on
#      them; making this an assertion would gate a real pin on an unrelated
#      Armbian UI decision.
ARMBIAN_BOARDS="rock-5b-plus orangepi5-plus"
ARMBIAN_BOARDFAMILY="rockchip-rk3588"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Resolved kernel coordinates for BRANCH=bleedingedge.
#
# Resolution chain (all four files at ARMBIAN_BUILD_REV):
#   1. config/sources/families/rockchip-rk3588.conf sources rockchip64_common.inc
#      on line 10, then handles ONLY `legacy` and `vendor` in its own `case $BRANCH`.
#      `bleedingedge` therefore keeps whatever rockchip64_common.inc set. (Same
#      trap the `edge` derivation had: reading the family config alone suggests
#      the branch is unsupported.)
#   2. config/sources/families/include/rockchip64_common.inc `bleedingedge)` =>
#         KERNEL_MAJOR_MINOR="7.2"   LINUXFAMILY=rockchip64
#         LINUXCONFIG='linux-rockchip64-'$BRANCH
#      LINUXCONFIG is an INTERPOLATION, not a literal — it is the branch name
#      spliced in, which is why KERNEL_CONFIG_SOURCE below reads
#      `...-bleedingedge.config` and why preflight.sh expands $BRANCH rather than
#      string-matching a fixed name.
#   3. config/sources/mainline-kernel.conf.sh sets an EXPLICIT KERNELBRANCH for
#      7.2 (see KERNELBRANCH_ARMBIAN), so the rolling `branch:linux-<mm>.y`
#      default in `__900_defaults` never runs for this branch.
#   4. That same file then overrides KERNELSOURCE, keyed on the branch value it
#      just set (see KERNELSOURCE_ARMBIAN).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
KERNEL_MAJOR_MINOR="7.2"
LINUXFAMILY="rockchip64"

# Armbian's kernel config source for this branch. Verified present at
# ARMBIAN_BUILD_REV, not inferred from the LINUXCONFIG interpolation alone.
KERNEL_CONFIG_SOURCE="config/kernel/linux-rockchip64-bleedingedge.config"

# Armbian's own patch directory for this kernel family (where our series is
# dropped as userpatches/kernel/archive/rockchip64-7.2/).
KERNEL_PATCHDIR="patch/kernel/archive/rockchip64-7.2"

# The mainline source Armbian falls back to for a non-China region, from
# lib/functions/configuration/main-config.sh. This is also the remote every tag
# and SHA in this file was listed from.
KERNELSOURCE="https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/stable/linux.git"

# What Armbian ACTUALLY uses for 7.2 — an explicit RELEASE-CANDIDATE tag, not the
# rolling stable branch `edge`/7.1 used to get. Read out of
# config/sources/mainline-kernel.conf.sh at ARMBIAN_BUILD_REV:
#
#   mainline_kernel_decide_version__upstream_release_candidate_number()
#     if [[ "${KERNEL_MAJOR_MINOR}" == "7.2" ]]; then
#         declare -g KERNELBRANCH="tag:v7.2-rc7"
#
# The same file then redirects the source, keyed on that exact branch string:
#
#   mainline_kernel_decide_version__750_use_torvalds_for_7.2-rc7()
#     if [[ "${KERNELBRANCH}" == 'tag:v7.2-rc7' ]]; then
#         declare -g KERNELSOURCE="https://github.com/torvalds/linux.git"
#
# That redirect is an emergency hook for a tag kernel.org had not yet mirrored.
# It no longer describes reality — `v7.2-rc7` resolves on linux-stable today
# (checked 2026-08-26) — but it is recorded because it IS what the pinned rev
# does, and preflight.sh asserts both halves so an Armbian edit to either is
# caught rather than silently absorbed.
KERNELBRANCH_ARMBIAN="tag:v7.2-rc7"
KERNELSOURCE_ARMBIAN="https://github.com/torvalds/linux.git"

# (iii) WE PIN THE FINAL RELEASE, NOT ARMBIAN'S RELEASE CANDIDATE — DELIBERATELY.
#
# Armbian's 7.2 arm still points at `tag:v7.2-rc7` because its @TODO to roll over
# once 7.2 shipped has not been actioned. `v7.2` final was tagged 2026-08-16,
# one day BEFORE ARMBIAN_BUILD_REV, so the rc is simply stale — and pinning a
# release candidate would mean gating this series against a kernel nobody will
# ever run. We take the final tag; the MAJOR.MINOR (and therefore the config and
# patch-directory names above) is identical either way, which is what makes the
# substitution safe.
#
# Resolved 2026-08-26 by listing three remotes live — kernel.org linux-stable,
# gregkh/linux and torvalds/linux — never from a cache or a previous note. All
# three returned BOTH SHAs below, byte-identical.
#
# Both are pinned, and scripts/apply.sh asserts both. The peeled commit alone
# cannot see a tag OBJECT that was re-created — re-signed, re-dated, re-worded —
# while still pointing at the same commit, and the tag object is what a signature
# is verified against. Bump the two together or not at all.
KERNEL_TAG="v7.2"
KERNEL_TAG_OBJECT="237a1c39e8dfd3e1c6f1f023eea37a48ec04cc63"      # refs/tags/v7.2 (annotated tag object, tagged 2026-08-16)
KERNEL_COMMIT="8d3ae59288f1e7d58d76558a6ee96d533bc5019f"          # v7.2^{commit}

# GitHub mirror of linux-stable. Same objects, much faster in CI than kernel.org.
# Re-verified 2026-08-26: this mirror DOES carry `v7.2`, with the same tag object
# and the same peeled commit as kernel.org. An earlier note in this effort had
# expected it to lag and to need a torvalds fallback; it does not, so no fallback
# is configured. Re-check on the next bump rather than assuming either way.
KERNEL_MIRROR="https://github.com/gregkh/linux.git"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Upstream patch source (the fork parent).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
UPSTREAM_PATCHES_REPO="https://github.com/rcawston/rockchip-rk3588-mainline-patches"
UPSTREAM_PATCHES_REV="e13a311d8ee5e8ed92ec3d4a57c21f766c61d660"   # main @ 2026-07-01
# The kernel the upstream author states the raw patches were developed/tested on.
UPSTREAM_TESTED_KERNEL="v6.19-rc8"

# Kernel config symbol the VEPU580 encoder driver needs.
RKVENC_CONFIG_SYMBOL="CONFIG_VIDEO_ROCKCHIP_RKVENC"
